course: dictionary
lesson: 15713
title: Townsfolk: people of the town
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Folk means people, and a town is a place where many people live close together. Put them together and you get townsfolk: the people who live in a particular town. «The townsfolk gathered in the square to hear the news.» The clue is gathered. People come together in one place, so the townsfolk must be the people of that town.
explain: Townsfolk is a plural word, so it takes plural verbs such as were and gathered. Say «The townsfolk were excited», not «The townsfolk was excited». Townsfolk also means town people in general, so it sets them against country people. «The farmers came down from the hills to sell eggs to the townsfolk.» The farmers live in the country, and the townsfolk live in the town.
explain: In a story, the townsfolk are the ordinary people of the town. Old tales often use the word, and it can sound a little old-fashioned. In newspapers you will more often see townspeople or residents, which mean much the same thing. Use the context to check: who is gathering, who is afraid, who is celebrating?
fact: Townsfolk means the people who live in a particular town.
fact: Townsfolk is a plural noun, so use it with were, not was.
fact: «Townsfolk» is often used in stories, fairy tales and history, and it sounds a little old-fashioned.
fact: «Townspeople» and «residents» mean much the same thing, and they are more common in the news.
fact: Phrases you may meet include «the local townsfolk» and «the townsfolk gathered».
exercise: «The townsfolk gathered in the square to hear the news.» Who are the townsfolk? | Soldiers from a faraway country | Wandering travellers who never stay anywhere | *The people who live in the town | Animals that live in the forest
exercise: Which word in the news means nearly the same as townsfolk? | Pirates | Caves | Mountains | *Townspeople
exercise: «The farmers came down from the hills, and the townsfolk waited in the market.» Who lives in the country? | The market traders | *The farmers | Nobody, because the hills are empty | The townsfolk
test: Which sentence uses townsfolk correctly? | The townsfolk was happy to see the parade | *The townsfolk were happy to see the parade | The townsfolk happily sleeping in the parade | The townsfolk is happy to see the parade
test: What does townsfolk mean? | Old buildings in a town centre | Visitors who come for a single day | Wild animals that live in the town | *People who live in a particular town
exam: Which word is a close synonym of townsfolk when you read the news? | Sailors | *Residents | Castles | Caves
